    hold_none2: 'none',
    hold_g2: 'G2 or G3',
    hold_g3_nosame: 'G3 without the "same person" clue',
    hold_read: 'When one fraud in twenty pays such a seller 20 % or more of its money, G2 precision is {x} ({y} in the world where only 30 % of the money reaches exchanges). At one fraud in ten it is {z} for a seller and {w} for an ordinary customer. In every scenario tested up to one fraud in ten, G2 stays above the €10,000 rule. G3 is {g3} as long as no innocent is paid by the same fraud at two exchanges; it falls to {g3s} if 5 % of frauds pay such a seller. The sharing protocol adds up what one person receives at several exchanges: this finds more involved accounts, and it is also what flags a seller who works at two exchanges. Without the "same person" clue, G3 stays at {g3n} and finds {fn} of the involved accounts instead of {f3}. We keep the rule as it was frozen and show both.',
    hold_real: 'On-chain ({n} scam addresses that reach an exchange), {a} have at least one deposit account receiving 20 % or more of their money, and {b} have two accounts at 10 % or more at two different exchanges. We cannot tell involved accounts from innocent ones on-chain, so these are upper bounds, within two hops. The {b} counts two accounts with at least 10 % each; an uneven split (15 % and 5 %) is not counted.',
