    hold_size_note: 'Why {d} here and {w} in world 4 of Table 2, with the same amounts? World 4 and the first row keep the simulator\'s basic P2P buyers: half of the frauds pay 2 to 10 innocent buyers, €300 to €3,000 each, counted as false alerts. The sample rows replace them with the resale set in this table, at 0 % in the first two. Counting the P2P buyers as cases to check, world 4 is at {wc}.',
    hold_size_read: 'With the amounts of our phishing sample, G2 raises about as few false alerts per fraud as in our worlds ({fa} and {fb}, against {fa0}). Its precision is lower ({pA} and {pB}) because small frauds pay fewer involved accounts that can be traced: there are fewer right alerts beside the same few wrong ones. Peer-to-peer resale adds false alerts as more frauds use it: {f10}, {f25} and {f50} per fraud when 10, 25 and 50 % of frauds resell a fifth of their money to one buyer.',
    hold_g3_read: 'G3 stays at {g3} or more in all {n} cases of this test but one extreme scenario: an innocent buyer present at two exchanges and paid by one fraud in ten ({g3x}). It is the limit already shown in Table 4.',
